/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  evaporator11_naoh_olsson_50wt  --  real caustic duty, 20 -> 50 wt % NaOH

  The worked single-effect evaporator of Geankoplis, Transport Processes and
  Separation Process Principles, 5th ed., Example 32.4-3 (pp. 1502-1505):
  4536 kg/h of 20 wt % NaOH entering at 60 C, saturated steam at 172.4 kPa
  (115.6 C), U = 1560 W/m2/K, concentrated to 50 wt %.  The book FIXES the
  vapour-space pressure (11.7 kPa) and the product (50 %) and finds the area
  (49.2 m2) and the steam (3255 kg/h) from its Duhring and enthalpy-
  concentration charts.  This case RATES that design: it gives the unit the
  book's area, U and steam, and the engine finds the boiling temperature,
  the vessel pressure and the product concentration.  The book's printed
  answers are anchored in `expected` (kind: printed; they are chart
  readings, so the anchors carry the chart's reading tolerance).

  The solution is priced on `model OlssonNaOH;` (Olsson, Jernqvist & Aly,
  Int. J. Thermophys. 18 (1997) 779): water activity from their vapour-
  pressure correlation, the heat of dilution from their enthalpy
  correlation -- valid to 80 wt %, where the Na-OH Pitzer pair is fitted to
  6 mol/kg (19 wt %) and a 50 % liquor is 25 mol/kg.  Read the run for:

      heat of dilution = X kW   (L_phi: m 6.25 -> ~25 mol/kg salt, ...)
      BPE = ...                 (the EXACT a_w Psat(T) = Psat(T - BPE))

  and contrast evaporator08 (Pitzer, inside its 6 mol/kg window).

  Steam: 115.6 C is the book's saturation temperature; P is this case's own
  water Psat at that temperature, so the chest sits ON the package's
  saturation curve (the catalogue Antoine puts 172.4 kPa at 114.5 C).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        evap;
        type        evaporator;
        inputs      ( feed  steam );
        outputs     ( concentrated  vapour  condensate );
        operation
        {
            area        49.2 m2;
            U         1560.0 W/m2/K;
        }
    }
);
